          "heading": "Process Simulation & Industrial Design Case: Boundary Layer Theory, Drag Coefficients and Frictional Losses",
          "content": "# Applied Project 3 \u2014 Heat transport\n\nDevelop an engineering model. Supply geometry, conservation equations, units, two operating points, simulation plots, uncertainty, and a validation plan.\n\n## Detailed project brief\n\nEvaluate insulation on a hot vessel jacket: the inner and outer radii are 50 and 130 mm, jacket length is 1 m, insulation conductivity is 0.08 W/(m K), and the hot/cold interface temperatures are 160 and 35 \u00b0C. First compute radial conductive loss from known boundary temperatures. Next add a realistic outer convection resistance, explicitly noting that the outer surface temperature becomes an unknown and therefore the original boundary data cannot be imposed simultaneously. Compare two insulation thicknesses and identify any geometry-related nonlinear behavior. Consider a radiation component only after choosing an emissivity and enclosure temperature. Show why a planar slab estimate is biased for a thick radial layer and summarize the dominant uncertainty."
