          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Boundary Layer Theory, Drag Coefficients and Frictional Losses",
          "content": "# Unit 3 Assessment \u2014 Heat transport\n\n1. Explain the physical principle supporting Fourier law and plane-wall conduction and state all assumptions in q = kA(T_hot\u2212T_cold)/L.\n2. Derive a dimensionless or limiting-form check of q = kA(T_hot\u2212T_cold)/L; what happens when the driving gradient tends to zero?\n3. Design an experiment to distinguish a boundary-condition error from an incorrect material coefficient for Fourier law and plane-wall conduction.\n4. Explain the physical principle supporting Cylindrical and composite conduction and state all assumptions in R_cyl = ln(r\u2082/r\u2081)/(2\u03c0kL).\n5. Derive a dimensionless or limiting-form check of R_cyl = ln(r\u2082/r\u2081)/(2\u03c0kL); what happens when the driving gradient tends to zero?\n6. Design an experiment to distinguish a boundary-condition error from an incorrect material coefficient for Cylindrical and composite conduction.\n7. Explain the physical principle supporting Convection and transient cooling and state all assumptions in T(t)\u2212T\u221e = (T\u2080\u2212T\u221e)exp[\u2212hAt/(\u03c1Vc\u209a)].\n8. Derive a dimensionless or limiting-form check of T(t)\u2212T\u221e = (T\u2080\u2212T\u221e)exp[\u2212hAt/(\u03c1Vc\u209a)]; what happens when the driving gradient tends to zero?\n9. Design an experiment to distinguish a boundary-condition error from an incorrect material coefficient for Convection and transient cooling.\n10. Explain the physical principle supporting Thermal radiation and state all assumptions in q = \u03b5\u03c3A(T_s\u2074\u2212T_sur\u2074).\n11. Derive a dimensionless or limiting-form check of q = \u03b5\u03c3A(T_s\u2074\u2212T_sur\u2074); what happens when the driving gradient tends to zero?\n12. Design an experiment to distinguish a boundary-condition error from an incorrect material coefficient for Thermal radiation."
