          "id": "sec-5-7",
          "secNumber": "5.7",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Convective Heat Transfer, Dimensionless Numbers and Thermal Boundary Layers",
          "content": "# Unit 5 Assessment \u2014 Boundary layers\n\n1. Explain the physical principle supporting Laminar momentum boundary layer and state all assumptions in \u03b4/x \u2248 5/\u221aRe_x.\n2. Derive a dimensionless or limiting-form check of \u03b4/x \u2248 5/\u221aRe_x; what happens when the driving gradient tends to zero?\n3. Design an experiment to distinguish a boundary-condition error from an incorrect material coefficient for Laminar momentum boundary layer.\n4. Explain the physical principle supporting Thermal boundary layer and state all assumptions in \u03b4_T/\u03b4 \u2248 Pr^(\u22121/3).\n5. Derive a dimensionless or limiting-form check of \u03b4_T/\u03b4 \u2248 Pr^(\u22121/3); what happens when the driving gradient tends to zero?\n6. Design an experiment to distinguish a boundary-condition error from an incorrect material coefficient for Thermal boundary layer.\n7. Explain the physical principle supporting Concentration boundary layer and state all assumptions in \u03b4_C/\u03b4 \u2248 Sc^(\u22121/3).\n8. Derive a dimensionless or limiting-form check of \u03b4_C/\u03b4 \u2248 Sc^(\u22121/3); what happens when the driving gradient tends to zero?\n9. Design an experiment to distinguish a boundary-condition error from an incorrect material coefficient for Concentration boundary layer.\n10. Explain the physical principle supporting Similarity and transfer coefficients and state all assumptions in Nu = hL/k; Sh = k_c L/D.\n11. Derive a dimensionless or limiting-form check of Nu = hL/k; Sh = k_c L/D; what happens when the driving gradient tends to zero?\n12. Design an experiment to distinguish a boundary-condition error from an incorrect material coefficient for Similarity and transfer coefficients."
