        {
          "id": "sec-6-8",
          "secNumber": "6.8",
          "heading": "Experimental Quality Assurance, Transport Measurement & Error Protocol",
          "content": "Laboratory manual  The laboratory environment is fully local: no tracking, paid API or network dependency. Each model lists assumptions and solves an analytic expression or an explicitly labeled numerical approximation. Graphs show a physical profile and parameter sensitivity.  How to investigate   Copy the equation and state variable units.  Derive a limiting or conservation check.  Change only one input, then record both plots and the key numerical output.  Export the CSV and independently reproduce a calculation.  Identify an assumption that could fail in practice and an experiment to test it.   Safety and scope  All property and equipment data in this book are illustrative and intended solely for learning. None of the models selects relief equipment, predicts hazardous releases, sizes industrial units or replaces measured properties and qualified process-safety review. \n\n# Engineering measurement, uncertainty and model validation\n\n## Why equations need measured inputs\n\nAn equation can be mathematically exact under its assumptions and still predict a real process poorly if its inputs were measured incorrectly. In transport calculations, viscosity, effective diffusivity, thermal conductivity and boundary conditions can all carry uncertainty. Before comparing model and experiment, list each measured quantity, its units, calibration source and expected accuracy. Keep instrument resolution separate from repeatability: repeated readings may agree closely while every reading shares the same calibration bias.\n\n### Case A: pipe radius controls flow\n\nFor a pressure-controlled laminar circular pipe, the Hagen\u2013Poiseuille relation has Q proportional to R to the fourth power. Taking logarithmic differentials gives approximately \u03b4Q/Q = 4\u03b4R/R + \u03b4(\u0394p)/(\u0394p) \u2212 \u03b4\u03bc/\u03bc \u2212 \u03b4L/L for small signed changes. If radius is uncertain by \u00b12% while all else is treated as exact, its first-order contribution alone is roughly \u00b18% in Q. This is why reporting a very precise flow from a loosely measured pipe diameter creates false confidence. In a real laboratory, measure diameter at multiple locations and inspect noncircularity; the physical pipe may not have a single well-defined radius.\n\n### Case B: heat through a layered wall\n\nFor known surface temperatures, total steady heat rate through resistances in series is q=\u0394T/\u03a3R. If insulation conductivity is temperature dependent, evaluating k at one arbitrary temperature can systematically bias q. A physically appropriate estimate may integrate k(T) or iterate layer temperatures with measured property data. Contact resistances introduce additional drops that are absent from a perfect-contact wall model. Measure surface temperatures on both sides of each contact where possible; otherwise infer the combined thermal resistance and state that it may not be uniquely separable into component properties.\n\n### Case C: diffusivity from a release experiment\n\nA one-dimensional Gaussian tracer pulse offers an experimental estimate of effective diffusivity because its variance grows linearly as 2Dt. Suppose measured variance increases from 0.002 m\u00b2 at 10 s to 0.014 m\u00b2 at 70 s. The estimated slope is (0.014\u22120.002)/(70\u221210)=0.0002 m\u00b2/s, hence D=0.0001 m\u00b2/s. This conclusion assumes a uniform infinite medium without advection, reaction or unmodeled mixing. If the mean position also shifts, convective transport must be considered. A single measurement of peak concentration cannot reliably identify both injected mass and diffusivity.\n\n### Case D: boundary-layer coefficient identification\n\nA reported convective coefficient h is often computed as measured heat rate divided by exposed area and temperature difference. If the actual wall temperature varies across the surface, substituting a single thermocouple value may be inaccurate. In an external-flow heated-plate trial, place sensors at multiple downstream distances. Repeat at varied mean velocity and compare Nusselt trends with a correlation whose Reynolds and Prandtl limits match the equipment. Surface contamination, radiant exchange and conduction through the support can all masquerade as convection. Correcting for these parasitic paths is necessary before interpreting h as a clean fluid transfer coefficient.\n\n### Case E: numerical uncertainty versus model uncertainty\n\nA computational solver can exhibit excellent grid convergence while its material-property model is wrong. Suppose a refined model predicts 12.0 W, a still finer mesh predicts 11.9 W, but the only experimentally measured heat flow is 9.5 W. The mesh difference is 0.1 W, yet the model\u2013experiment discrepancy is 2.4 W. Refining the mesh further is unlikely to resolve a missing convection resistance or a conductivity error. Check residuals, boundary settings and energy conservation first, then challenge physical assumptions and measurements separately. State which uncertainty sources were quantified and which remain qualitative.\n\n## Practical uncertainty propagation\n\nFor a prediction y=f(x\u2081,\u2026,x\u2099) with small independent input uncertainties, one common first-order method uses u_y\u00b2\u2248\u03a3(\u2202f/\u2202x_i)\u00b2u_i\u00b2. For correlated inputs there are additional covariance terms. The approximation may fail with strongly nonlinear responses, broad uncertainties, discontinuous regimes or parameters near physical limits. Monte Carlo sampling can help if plausible input distributions are available, but arbitrary normal distributions must not be treated as evidence. In all uncertainty analyses, specify whether quoted intervals are standard uncertainties, expanded uncertainties or observed ranges.\n\n## Engineering reporting checklist\n\nA reproducible transport calculation should make it possible for a separate reader to reconstruct its main result. Record the domain geometry; draw the direction of positive flux; write all governing laws and their units; identify every boundary and initial condition; document material properties and their sources; and state any empirical correlation and validity limits. Include at least one independent limiting-case calculation, such as zero gradient causing zero pure conductive flux or a vanishing reaction rate giving nearly complete catalyst utilization. Report numerical solver settings if applicable, including grid resolution, timestep and residual definition. Finally, distinguish what was directly measured, what was fitted, what was computed and what was inferred. This separation prevents a simplified teaching simulation from being mistaken for a validated industrial design tool."
        }
