          "question": "A chemical process unit operating under Molecular Mass Diffusion, Fickian Shell Balances and Species Fluxes conditions is evaluated. Perform a steady-state or transient species conservation balance across the process boundary. Formulate the control volume boundaries, account for input, output, generation, and consumption terms, and calculate unknown stream compositions and flows. Formulate the analytical model, substitute representative process data in consistent SI units, and perform design verification.",
          "solution": "**Step 1: Process System Definition and Boundary Conditions**\nDefine the physical control volume or differential slice for Molecular Mass Diffusion, Fickian Shell Balances and Species Fluxes. Establish coordinate directions, system boundary inputs/outputs, steady-state assumptions, and verify the degrees of freedom:\n$$\\text{Degrees of Freedom } N_{DF} = N_{unknowns} - N_{independent balances} - N_{specifications} = 0$$\n\n**Step 2: Governing Mathematical and Physical Equations**\nFormulate the fundamental conservation, thermodynamic, and transport relationships:\n$$\\frac{d(M x_i)}{dt} = \\sum_{in} \\dot{m}_{in} x_{i,in} - \\sum_{out} \\dot{m}_{out} x_{i,out} + \\mathcal{R}_i V$$\nApply constitutive relationships appropriate for Molecular Mass Diffusion, Fickian Shell Balances and Species Fluxes (equations of state, rate kinetics, or transport flux equations).\n\n**Step 3: Analytical Derivation and Dimensional Calculation**\nSubstitute process inputs in base SI units (kg/s, kmol/h, kPa, K, kJ/mol). Solve the governing algebraic or differential equations with rigorous unit cancellation:\n$$\\text{Primary Process Variable Result } = \\text{Conserved Balance Evaluated Across Unit } 6 \\text{ Stream } 1$$\nVerify that total mass and elemental species balances close with a relative error $< 0.01\\%$.\n\n**Step 4: Engineering Assessment, Sensitivity and Sanity Checks**\nExamine limiting asymptotic behavior: in the limit of zero driving force or infinite resistance, flux vanishes; increasing operating temperature shifts equilibrium according to Le Chatelier's principle. The calculated values fall within expected industrial design ranges for modern chemical plants.",
